isabelle mcp_server — changelog
================================

Design decisions and milestones, newest first. The spec is the full
record; entries here point at the section that carries the details.

2026-07-22 (symbol recoding at the client edge: the model sees ⟹, not \<Longrightarrow>)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- decision (spec: "symbol recoding at the client edge"): isabelle's
  convention is ML speaks symbol notation (\<Longrightarrow>,
  \<open>...\<close>) and scala speaks unicode (⟹, ‹...›), and
  isabelle/scala enforces it in Pure/PIDE/prover.scala — but only on
  the NON-protocol channel: message_output branches to protocol_output
  before applying Symbol.decode, and protocol_command_raw skips the
  Symbol.encode_yxml that protocol_command_args does. the mcp bridge
  rides the protocol channel exclusively (protocol_command_raw for
  MCP.ir, MCP.tools, MCP.run_tool, MCP.read_resource,
  MCP.check_designation), so it skipped BOTH recodings and carried raw
  ML-side symbol notation all the way to the mcp client — every proof
  state, every error, every resource read arrived spelled
  \<Longrightarrow>. the server now recodes itself, at the client edge:
  exactly one point in each direction, mirroring phase 1's "yxml on the
  bridge, json only on the client edge". this qualifies rather than
  contradicts the "protocol-command chunks are byte-clean" note in the
  ml-bridge section: byte-cleanliness on the wire is real and still
  holds; it was never the same thing as speaking the client's alphabet.
- outbound: Symbol.decode in MCP_Server.text_result and
  MCP_Server.resource_contents. at the client edge rather than at the
  per-chunk parse sites because it catches every string regardless of
  origin, and Symbol.decode is idempotent on already-unicode text (its
  recoder rewrites only \-initiated sequences and its own output
  contains none) — verified in Pure/General/symbol.scala's Recoder and
  pinned by a test, so content that arrived via the DECODED channel
  (render_messages over snapshot messages) is unharmed by the second
  pass.
- inbound: recode = Symbol.encode on the YXML.string_of_body calls in
  MCP_Session.encode_args and encode_names, so the model may send
  either alphabet and both reach ML as symbol notation. it goes in as
  that parameter, never as a pass over an assembled chunk:
  string_of_body applies recode to TEXT NODES ONLY (yxml.scala,
  Output_String.string), whereas Symbol.encode over finished yxml would
  walk its X/Y control bytes.
- per-call-site audit of the other protocol_command_raw arguments: the
  bare Bytes(...) values are deliberately left unencoded. request ids
  are UUIDs; designations are repl ids, theory long names or bundle
  names; tool and resource names are the EXPOSED mcp names, which the
  mcp name charset already restricts to [A-Za-z0-9_-]. all ascii by
  construction, so Symbol.encode would be a no-op on them and adding it
  would only obscure where the real boundary is. the one argument that
  can carry model-authored term text is the run_tool / ir payload, and
  that is encoded because it rides encode_args.
- CONSEQUENCE, recorded rather than hidden: the client-edge property
  weakens from byte identity to identity up to symbol normalization —
  send \<Longrightarrow>, get ⟹ back. byte identity still holds
  strictly BELOW the decode point, which is where the existing fidelity
  tests assert it (plans/repl_text T1, plans/repl_step T1 — ml unit and
  bridge level, both of which read backend results directly and never
  touch text_result); confirmed green, unchanged. text holding a
  literal \<foo> that was meant verbatim comes back as the glyph.
- known limitations, accepted and not fixed here: \<^sub> / \<^sup> /
  \<^bold> decode to the marker glyphs ⇩ / ⇧ / ❙ (U+21E9/U+21E7/U+2759)
  rather than typeset sub/superscripts — jEdit does that with font
  styling and unicode cannot express it; ~72 of the 512 etc/symbols
  entries have no code: field (mostly \<^const>, \<^cterm>, the ml
  antiquotation controls) and pass through decode untouched, which is
  desirable — byte precision preserved exactly where it matters; and
  tools/list / resources/list DESCRIPTIONS bypass both text_result and
  resource_contents, so symbol notation inside an mcp_tool's
  `description \<open>...\<close>` still reaches the client raw, as
  does the backend-derived message on resources/read's RPC.error
  branch. those last two are real remaining sites, left open
  deliberately as a separate scope decision rather than widened in
  silently.
- tests: new MCP_Symbol_Tests (scala unit, 10 tests) covering
  Symbol.decode idempotence on already-unicode text, Symbol.encode as a
  no-op on already-symbol text, the control-symbol marker glyphs and
  their exact encode round trip, code-less symbols passing through, the
  two outbound edges (text_result including its is_error path, and
  resource_contents), and the end-to-end tools/call round trip in both
  alphabets. MCP_Codec_Tests's "symbols round-trip byte-clean" claim
  split in two, because encode_args no longer preserves unicode
  verbatim: ascii symbol notation still round-trips byte-identical, and
  unicode is asserted to arrive as symbol notation on the wire.
- verified: `isabelle mcp_test` green (the first run tripped the
  pre-existing 30s-timeout flake in MCP_Doc_Catalog_Tests's cold
  Isar_Ref scan; green in isolation and on re-run, unrelated to this
  change), `isabelle mcp_test -b` fully green including repl_text T1's
  byte-fidelity assertion, and mcp/test/test_mcp.py green end to end
  (0 failures) against a live server. note for future e2e runs through
  the flatpak: test_mcp.py's fixture dir comes from tempfile.mkdtemp,
  and the flatpak's private /tmp is invisible to the sandboxed prover —
  point TMPDIR at a path inside the repo or load_theory reports
  "Cannot load theory file" for reasons that have nothing to do with
  the code under test.

2026-07-16 (repl_init_from_source: command/segment-target attach lands)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

- milestone (wave 1, plan: plans/repl_init_from_source, status DONE):
  a new builtin tool creates a REPL rooted at a command inside an
  existing theory instead of a fresh import — {repl, theory, offset? |
  pattern? | index?}, exactly one locator, handler-enforced (schema
  can't express it). Loaded (PIDE-checked) theories resolve scala-side
  over a new shared MCP_Session.Locator (pure Item(id, offset, length,
  source) + exactly_one/resolve, unit-tested standalone with no
  backend or prover — the reusable half of "command-target resolution"
  the spec calls out, meant for goto_definition too) walking Document.
  Node.command_iterator, then dispatch to the existing
  "init_from_document" ir fname. Image theories with recorded segments
  have no PIDE document, so that branch is new ML instead (MCP_Repl.
  thy's init_from_segment, dispatcher fname "init_from_segment"):
  offset/pattern/index resolved against Thy_Info.get_theory_segments
  text with the same semantics (pattern = first segment containing it;
  offset = position in the segments' concatenated text, valid since
  segments partition the source contiguously), then forwarded to
  Ir.init as a literal "thy_name:idx" spec — the segment-text
  resolution genuinely can't be shared with the scala Locator (segment
  text only exists in the process that recorded it), so it is a
  separate implementation of the same shape, not a shared call. open
  question resolved: AFTER semantics were kept as specced (Ir.init_
  from_document's existing eval_result_state) — a bridge test attaches
  at a lemma statement and confirms the created REPL lands in the open
  goal state ("[proof]"), its own result, not its predecessor's; the
  mid-proof "attach at the failing command" workflow is client-side
  (pattern the command before the one to fix). T1/T2 (locator
  enforcement, pure resolver) are scala-unit (mcp_handler_tests.scala,
  MCP_Locator_Tests); T3..T5 (inaccessible-command status error,
  image-tier segment fallback, full loaded-tier attach/step/text/
  remove chain) are bridge tests against MCP-HOL's own -T MCP_Repl
  theory, reusing the same recorded-segments fixture the retracted
  segments-gap tests already established works live.

2026-07-16 (builtin activation unification: implemented)
----------------------------------------------------------

- milestone (spec phase 3 "builtin tools in the activation layer",
  decided 2026-07-13; plan: plans/builtin_activation, status DONE):
  every scala builtin now has an ML mirror row (MCP_Tool.form Builtin,
  tag "builtin", one folded MCP_Tool.declare pass at the end of
  MCP_Tools.thy; run slot errors "builtin tool: dispatched
  Isabelle/Scala-side" if ever invoked directly), so [[mcp_tools
  add/del: ...]], bundles, print_mcp_tools and tool_scope_* govern
  builtins exactly like user tools. mechanics: MCP.tools' wire shape
  grows from a flat row list to a PAIR (ml rows, builtins section);
  the builtins section is (base name, active) for every registered
  mirror, inactive included, so "hidden" (registered, del'd) is
  distinguishable from "absent" (no mirror at all). scala:
  MCP_Session.Tools_Reply(rows, builtin_activation) replaces the bare
  row list end to end (trait, promise, Fake_Backend); the merge rule
  hides a builtin iff its mirror is explicitly (name, false), so an
  empty or missing section hides nothing — the AVAILABILITY FLOOR
  falls out with no special-cased wire shape. Found and fixed live: the
  designation-resolution-failure branch of MCP.tools (already
  crash-safe since tool_scope) used to emit the bare empty list "[]";
  feeding that to a PAIR decoder would throw inside the promise-
  completing message handler and hang tools/list forever — the floor
  case now emits MCP_Protocol.empty_tools_body, a properly-shaped
  empty pair (caught before it could regress, via the bridge test that
  exercises an unknown designation). The exposure() RESERVED name set
  stays the FULL builtin table regardless of activation — only the
  LISTING is filtered — so a del'd builtin's bare name can never be
  grabbed by a same-named ML tool (ASYMMETRIC CALLABILITY holds: a
  del'd builtin is unlisted but dispatches exactly as before, since
  tools/call precedes activation entirely and is otherwise unchanged;
  a del'd ML tool drops out of MCP_Tool.active and is refused via
  exposed-name resolution failure, not "Inactive MCP tool" — that
  message is reachable only by dispatching the internal name directly,
  a distinction recorded as a spec refinement in the plan's A5). DRIFT
  GATE: a live-bridge test pins the ML mirror name set
  (MCP_Tools.thy's "Builtin tool mirrors" section) equal to the scala
  table's name set (new MCP_Server.all_builtin_names, the one
  authoritative list beyond `builtins`, covering the per-connection
  tool_scope_* trio too, since those three close over per-connection
  state and cannot be listed statically like `builtins`) — a builtin
  added scala-side without a mirror, or an orphaned mirror, now fails
  a test instead of silently drifting; a scala-unit test welds the
  hand-maintained tool_scope_* name literal to what a fresh Handler
  actually serves, closing the one blind spot the live-bridge gate
  can't see on its own (a new connection-state tool added without
  updating both the literal and the mirror). all four layers green:
  ml unit (MCP-Tools-Tests: mirror del/add round trip, run-slot error,
  hidden-vs-absent via the builtins section); scala unit
  (mcp_handler_tests.scala: empty-floor serves the full table, an
  explicit (name, false) hides exactly that builtin, (name, true) is a
  no-op, a hidden builtin still dispatches, the tool_scope literal
  matches a live Handler);
  bridge (mcp_bridge_tests.scala: the drift gate, and a repl-local del
  — declare [[mcp_tools del: ...]] inside one repl's context, never
  touching the shared MCP_Tools theory — leaves a builtin unlisted but
  callable under that repl's tool_scope_set); e2e (test_mcp.py, new
  test_builtin_activation: a del'd builtin (repl_list) stays callable
  though unlisted, a del'd ML tool is refused and unlisted; 95/95
  passing). spec's implementation-order item checked off with the
  landed shape recorded in detail.

2026-07-15 (SECONDARY resource-autonomy assumption: RESOLVED POSITIVE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- Tested the SECONDARY, MUST-BE-RESOLVED assumption from the
  "resource-read tool mirrors" spec section against claude code (the
  live client). RESULT: claude code DOES surface MCP resources to the
  model. It exposes model-invokable ListMcpResourcesTool /
  ReadMcpResourceTool / ReadMcpResourceDirTool; a mid-turn test listed
  the isabelle server's resources and read isabelle://session and
  isabelle://named/greeting with no human @-mention of any uri.
- Consequence: the resource surface is NOT locked into a human-in-the-
  loop-only workflow — in claude code an unattended agent reaches
  resources/list and resources/read directly. This RELIEVES (does not
  eliminate) the pressure toward the mirror tools; the mirror decision
  below still stands for clients that expose resources to the human
  only and for a fully-autonomous mode that prefers a tool surface.
- Spec updated in place: the SECONDARY bullet now reads RESOLVED
  POSITIVE with the evidence.

2026-07-15 (resource-read tool mirrors: decided + planned)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- Resolved a design concern the user raised: why run BOTH resources and
  tools, and are we using resources for the problem they solve. Answer
  recorded in the spec's "resource-read tool mirrors" section. MCP's
  primitives split by CONTROL: tools are model-invoked, resources are
  application/user-invoked (canonically a human picks them). Our
  tool/resource CUT is sound; the mismatch was AUDIENCE — we aimed a
  read surface (annotations.audience ["assistant"], lazy/scoped/sliced)
  at the model, on a primitive whose designed consumer is the human.
- Two operating assumptions now recorded, interlocked:
  (1) PRIMARY, human-in-the-loop — the main usage on a ~1-year horizon
  is a human directing the model through claude code; the user
  @-mentions resources when needed, so autonomous resource-reading is
  NOT required for this mode.
  (2) SECONDARY, UNVERIFIED, MUST BE RESOLVED — we assume claude code
  lets the model enumerate/read resources autonomously. Named check:
  does the client surface resources to the model on its own, or only
  via user @server:uri? Negative resolution forces expose-by-default
  vs. mirrors-active-by-default.
- Decision: build a builtin TOOL mirror for every readable resource, so
  a fully-autonomous-proving mode can hide the resource surface and
  serve the equivalent tools (model-invoked) instead. The mirror set:
  read_theory, read_commands, read_diagnostics, list_entities,
  read_session, read_named. isabelle://repl/{id} and /text need no
  mirror — repl_show/repl_text already are the twins. This elevates the
  2026-07-12 "flagged, NOT scheduled" note to DECIDED/planned (wave
  TBD). Mirrors ship registered-but-inactive; a bundle
  autonomous_proving = [[mcp_tools add: ...]] activates them, riding the
  phase-3 activation layer (this does NOT contradict "resources are NOT
  mirrored" there — that forbids mirroring resource TEMPLATES; these are
  first-class tools). Resource-suppression mechanism is the one new
  piece, DEFERRED to the plan. Invariant: one uri-handler function, two
  surfaces — a mirror never grows behavior its resource lacks.
- Plans: plans/resource_tool_mirrors (umbrella) + per-tool
  plans/read_theory, plans/read_commands, plans/read_diagnostics,
  plans/list_entities, plans/read_session, plans/read_named;
  plans/README updated; the implementation-order back-reference
  (find_theorems item) corrected.

2026-07-14 (recap planned; theory-not-repl refinement)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- plans/recap written for the spec's "recap theory" section (still
  unscheduled). Two decisions recorded there.
  (1) ML ONLY, no scala: the engine (fact diff, statement printing,
  oracle/axiom audit) is prover-side under every option, and the
  mcp_tool command already registers an ml_run tool whose (status,
  TEXT) reply IS recap's output contract. A scala builtin would still
  need an ML mirror row (plans/builtin_activation) and still cross to
  ML to do the work; it buys only "listed without importing the recap
  theory", which an end-of-session action does not need. Promoting it
  later is mechanical.
  (2) SPEC REFINEMENT — recap targets THEORIES, not repls. The spec's
  mechanism sketch says "session/repl id in"; a repl id is unsound.
  A generated Recap.thy is a file that imports on-disk theories and
  can only `by (fact ...)` facts those files define. A repl's steps
  live in a transient theory that no file exports, so a recap naming
  them cannot build. Input is a theory-name list (Thy_Info.get_theory,
  explicit param, not the ambient tool_scope designation); a repl
  target is REFUSED with the reason, and the honest loop is persist ->
  load_theory -> recap. Fold back into the spec when recap is
  scheduled.
  Design priority pinned (user, 2026-07-14): the deliverable is SOURCE
  an Isabelle developer audits by eye, not an agent's prose account of
  what it proved. Every claim in the generated file is a checked Isar
  construct (`by (fact ...)`, @{thm}/@{const}, the imports clause);
  the only natural language allowed is a requirement's own linking
  prose and the trust audit's statements of fact. An entry that cannot
  be restated honestly is commented out with the reason, never
  paraphrased past.
  Also recorded: recap's failure mode is completeness, not soundness
  (imperfect printing makes `by (fact)` fail the build, loudly; a
  false claim can never build), which is what justifies the spec's
  "completely automatic" optimism -- with the trust audit (oracles /
  sorry / axioms) closing the one hole, a green build resting on a
  skipped proof.

2026-07-14 (doc_read implemented)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- plans/doc_read landed (wave 5, second of the pair): Doc_Catalog
  (mcp/src/doc_catalog.scala) gained the toc/section/plain-window
  machinery, all pure functions of file paths so they are unit-tested
  directly against the bundled distribution's real doc sources (no
  fake catalog, no headless session) -- Heading scan is D1a, the
  line-anchored regex over "command \<open>Title \label{l}\<close>", since
  the outer-syntax-span spike (D1b) never landed; T6 pins a raw
  line-start count as the canary against a future distribution
  reformatting headings across lines. D3 revised during
  implementation: there was no existing ?lines=/window-cap code to
  reuse anywhere in the tree (isabelle://theory/{name} reads whole
  files today) -- doc_read is the first tool to implement the spec's
  oversized-read rule, so it establishes Doc_Catalog.window_lines =
  200 as the cap rather than reusing one. New builtin tool doc_read
  {name, section?, lines?} in mcp_server.scala, scala-only (no
  MCP.ir, no ML); section and lines are mutually exclusive
  (handler-enforced). T1..T7 all green (scala-unit against real
  Isar_Ref sources + NEWS, one bridge case for the full
  doc_list -> doc_read(toc) -> doc_read(section) chain).

2026-07-14 (doc_list implemented)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- plans/doc_list landed (wave 5, first of the pair): new
  mcp/src/doc_catalog.scala, Doc_Catalog.make(structure) a pure
  function of Sessions.Structure -- Doc.contents() sections joined to
  their doc-group source session via Info.document_variants (already
  parsed by Sessions.Structure into stripped variant names, so D1's
  planned manual ":"/"=" splitting turned out unnecessary -- verified
  against src/Doc/ROOT: document_variants = "isar-ref"/"logics-ZF"/
  "main" match the Contents entries exactly). D2 (ML_Settings.init()
  degrading to release_notes()++main_contents() on failure) landed as
  planned. New builtin tool doc_list {pattern?} in mcp_server.scala,
  scala-only (no MCP.ir, no ML), not scope-filtered. T1..T5 all green;
  T2 retargeted mid-implementation (see plans/doc_list) since the
  parsing claim it was written against turned out to already be
  Sessions.Structure's own correct behavior, not code this tool adds --
  retested against Doc_Catalog.join, the pure variant-name-to-session
  fold, instead.

2026-07-14 (documentation for the agent: manuals, requirements, recap)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- new spec section "documentation for the agent (manuals,
  requirements, recap)" under phase 2. three decisions:
- doc_list / doc_read (SCHEDULED, wave 5; plans/doc_list,
  plans/doc_read): the agent reads the manuals' theory SOURCES
  (src/Doc/<Manual>/*.thy — plain text, one file per chapter), never
  the pdfs. catalog from isabelle.Doc.contents() (doc/Contents),
  joined to source sessions through the startup session structure
  (src/Doc is on the top-level ROOTS; document_variants is the join
  key). tools, not resources — documentation lookup happens
  autonomously mid-proof. search_sources already covers grepping the
  manuals (doc sessions are ordinary sessions in the structure).
- requirements traceability (recorded, NOT scheduled): external pdfs
  stay client-side; the server owns the formal side of the link. a
  lightweight ML package — `requirement` command into theory data,
  [satisfies REQ] attribute, print_requirements diagnostic (exposed
  over MCP for free via mcp_tool) — plus the trust audit
  (thm_oracles/sorry/axiomatization). Isabelle/DOF studied and
  rejected as a dependency (heavyweight external component); its
  data-model shape is the template.
- recap theory (recorded, NOT scheduled): autonomous sessions end
  with a CHECKED Recap.thy — claimed theorems restated and proved
  `by (fact name)`, so `isabelle build` re-verifies existence AND
  statement; generation is a future `recap` builtin returning theory
  TEXT (client writes the file, per the editing policy), with the
  trust audit in the reply. doubles as ground truth for long tasks.
- two verified mechanisms folded in (research agent, same day): the
  Doc catalog is USER-EXTENSIBLE ($ISABELLE_DOCS + per-dir Contents
  file — a project's papers/ folder enters doc_list with zero server
  code, and @{doc your-paper} becomes build-time-checked); @{doc}
  cannot address a page/section (the markup chain carries none), so
  requirement source pointers get a custom @{doc_section} antiquotation
  (Doc.check + Markup.url with a hyperref fragment) when the
  traceability package is built.

2026-07-13 (bridge tests: theory-resource tier matrix)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- refactor (mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala): the 3 ad hoc
  "wave 2 resources" tests fixed just above tested tiers
  inconsistently -- each resource's test covered a different subset
  of the 4 tiers (image/loaded/filesystem/unrecognized), and the
  names doing duty as "filesystem tier" (Wave2Res*Never) were never
  real theory_map entries at all, so they silently tested the
  wholly-unrecognized case while the label claimed otherwise. That
  asymmetry is exactly how the fix above went unnoticed for two
  years' worth of history.
- replaced with a shared Tier_Expect/assert_tier helper and one
  matrix test per resource (diagnostics/entities/commands/bare
  source), each listing all 4 tiers explicitly so a missing or wrong
  case fails a single obvious assertion instead of going untested.
  tier_filesystem is now MCP-HOL-Tests.MCP_Fixture_Nav, a real
  on-disk theory from the sibling MCP-HOL-Tests session (known to
  this session's theory_map, never loaded here) -- this is also the
  first test to exercise theory_source_uri's FileSystemTier real-
  file-read code path with genuine content, previously untested even
  before today's fix.
- swept every other resolve_theory/tier-branch call site in
  mcp_session.scala (load_theory/check_theory's master_dir shortcut,
  resolve_context_theory) for the same class of bug: none found --
  those two are structurally different (an optional shortcut with a
  safe fallback, and a context selector where None is deliberately a
  hard error), so the fix and this matrix are scoped to the four
  theory-resource reads.

2026-07-13 (MCP-HOL.MCP: the HOL-anchored registration entry point)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- found debugging a user theory (ctree): importing the Pure-based
  MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools next to HOL-Library theories made the first
  codatatype raise SIMPLIFIER ("Congruence not a meta-equality",
  [case_cong]). Mechanism: Context.begin_thy's make_parents drops
  imports subsumed by other imports (Main is a proper subtheory of
  any HOL-Library theory), so the Pure-based registry silently
  becomes the FIRST merged parent; theory data folds left-to-right
  from the first parent and merge_ss keeps mk_rews (HOL's mk_cong,
  which meta-fies Trueprop-eq congruences) from the left argument
  only — the merged theory runs on Pure's cong setup and
  datatype/codatatype's add_eqcong dies. Bisected live through the
  server's own repl + load_theory tools; each link verified.
- decision: users never import MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools directly. New
  theory mcp/Tools/HOL/MCP.thy ("MCP-HOL.MCP", in the MCP-HOL
  session) imports Main FIRST and re-exports the mcp_tool/
  mcp_resource commands; since its own simpset carries HOL's
  mk_rews, it is safe in any import position. Verified: previously
  fatal first-position import + mcp_tool registration, and the exact
  ctree-shaped import list, both load clean through the server.
- README's extension examples now import MCP-HOL.MCP (with a comment
  naming the pitfall); the isabelle-ml skill's "Contexts and context
  data" topic records the parent-merge-order pitfall with MCP.thy as
  the local worked example.

2026-07-13 (distribution: component registration is the whole install)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

- decision: registering the mcp/ component must be the only setup a
  user ever does — no -d flags pointing into this repository, in any
  configuration. Mechanism: a top-level ROOTS catalog at mcp/ (one
  line, "Tools"); Isabelle's Sessions.load_structure merges every
  registered component directory that carries ROOT/ROOTS into the
  global session namespace (Sessions.directories, sessions.scala), so
  MCP-Tools/MCP-HOL become resolvable everywhere: isabelle build,
  the server's own build/resources/structure, and user ROOT files
  (which can now use MCP-HOL as a parent session directly).
- this exposed a latent bug: MCP_Session.start's "already in the
  image" check for the -T registry theory tried only the verbatim and
  running-session-qualified spellings, but the image keys theories by
  their DEFINING session ("MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools" in an MCP-HOL image).
  It only ever worked because the default -d made the .thy findable
  on disk for the use_theories fallback. Fixed with a unique
  base-name match over loaded_theories (same normalization as
  image_theory), for unqualified -T only.
- mcp-main's special default -d ($ISABELLE_MCP_HOME/Tools) is gone:
  redundant now, and it removes the "any -d replaces the default"
  footgun that silently broke MCP-HOL resolution the moment a user
  added their own project directory. README rewritten accordingly
  (install pre-build is a bare `isabelle build -b MCP-HOL`; .mcp.json
  example is `-s MCP-HOL -d .`).
- verified end to end against a live server: (a) no -d, unrelated
  cwd; (b) only `-d .` in a user project whose ROOT bases a session
  on MCP-HOL — user session appears in list_sessions next to ours;
  (c) ad-hoc user tool via repl_init/repl_step (mcp_tool in a repl) +
  tool_scope_set repl → served in tools/list and callable.
- KNOWN GAP (decision pending): tool_scope_set on a wave-2 LOADED
  theory (load_theory'd, not in the image) Scala-resolves fine but
  every ML-side use fails "Unknown theory ... in MCP designation" —
  document-model theories are not in Thy_Info, and document.ML
  exports no by-name theory access, so ML cannot build the context.
  Options: honest early error at tool_scope_set steering to the repl
  route; or real support by loading ML-side via Thy_Info.use_theories
  (Ir.load_theory exists, but needs name/master_dir plumbing across
  the bridge). The test sessions (MCP-Tools-Tests/MCP-HOL-Tests) also
  become globally visible through the catalog — cosmetic; splitting
  them into non-cataloged ROOT files touches the dev workflow
  (mcp_test -b fixtures, rebuild rule) and is left as a follow-up.

2026-07-13 (fix: theory resource reads for loaded/unrecognized names)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- found running the full bridge suite (isabelle mcp_test -b) while
  landing find_definition: three wave-3 resource tests failed
  (diagnostics/entities/commands+source for names outside the image
  tier), a regression latent since wave 3 library discovery
  (6293023c) itself — the tests were written against an intended
  optimistic contract ("resource reads report state, they don't fail
  just because a theory hasn't been loaded/indexed") that the
  implementation never actually matched for two cases: a name absent
  from theory_map entirely (no filesystem match, e.g. a typo) fell to
  a bare "Unknown theory" error instead of the same not-yet-backed
  text a real-but-unloaded filesystem theory gets; and a genuinely
  wave-2-loaded (non-image) theory's /commands and bare /theory read
  returned an Ok stub ("... not yet available for wave-2 theories")
  instead of the documented not-backed-yet error the loaded tier is
  supposed to give (it has no Thy_Info segments either way).
- fix: mcp_session.scala's theory_diagnostics/theory_entities merge
  the `None` (unrecognized name) case into their existing
  FileSystemTier arm (same optimistic text/error, since load_theory
  is the actionable next step regardless of which failure mode it
  turns out to be); theory_commands_uri/theory_source_uri merge
  LoadedTier and `None` into one "documented template, not backed
  yet" error, leaving FileSystemTier's own behavior (a real stub for
  commands, a real file read for source) untouched.
- verified: confirmed the 3 failures reproduced identically with this
  session's other changes (ROOT/find_definition) reverted, isolating
  them as pre-existing before fixing; full mcp_test and mcp_test -b
  are green after the fix.

2026-07-13 (find_definition: NAME-based lookup lands)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- milestone (spec "navigation"; plan: plans/find_definition, status
  DONE): the find_definition builtin looks a name up across the
  prover's authoritative name spaces (consts, types, classes, facts,
  locales, methods, attributes) instead of guessing from text, so it
  works for anything any command introduced. `kind` narrows the
  search to one space; omitted, all seven are searched and an
  ambiguous short name (e.g. "simp", both a method and an attribute,
  and registered by both Pure and HOL) returns every match rather
  than guessing one — a search, not a compile.
- context selectors (`repl` | `theory`, mutually exclusive, default
  the base image) reuse find_theorems' context-promotion machinery
  verbatim: MCP_Backend.resolve_context_theory normalizes the
  client's theory spelling scala-side before it crosses the bridge;
  the ML dispatcher only needs a Proof.context, so the repl branch is
  a plain Ir.context_of instead of find_theorems' own goal-aware
  last-state access.
- source-block enrichment: when the defining theory has recorded
  segments (record_theories=true, already-built heap), a hit carries
  the whole defining command text, not just kind/name/position — the
  same segment machinery entities/source already rely on. Verified
  live against a real MCP-HOL-Tests heap (isabelle mcp_server -s
  MCP-HOL-Tests): a fixture datatype/definition's hit text/1-1 in
  practice matched. A repl-inline definition's entry lives in an
  anonymous, Thy_Info-untracked theory value (Name_Space.theory_name
  reports the repl id itself, not a Thy_Info key), and a base heap
  theory built without record_theories (HOL.List) has no segments at
  all — both are graceful position-only, not an error.
- new ML surface lives in MCP_Repl.thy (find_definition_* functions),
  not ir.ML, which stays a verbatim reuse (Amazon-header constraint):
  this is the first plan to grow the ML surface as new code rather
  than a pass-through to Ir.
- test fixture: mcp/Tools/HOL/Tests/MCP_Fixture_Nav.thy, a small
  on-disk theory built with record_theories (MCP-HOL-Tests session
  now sets that option too, so its own theories carry segments once
  loaded fresh by a later process — never the production MCP-HOL
  heap the server loads).

2026-07-13 (scope_show: the read side of the resource scope lands)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- milestone (spec "scoping"; plan: plans/scope_show, status DONE): the
  scope_show builtin reads S1's state back out — the explicit patterns
  with their match counts, plus every implicit member: theories loaded
  via load_theory/check_theory, active REPLs, and registered named
  resources. Zero-arg like repl_list. No bridge of its own: patterns/
  theories/named resources were already scala-tracked state from
  scope_add/scope_remove; active REPL ids are read by reusing
  repl_list's own "repls" ir fname (MCP_Session.active_repl_ids())
  rather than adding a new one.
- this also closes scope_add's own dated deviation: active REPLs are
  now folded into resources/list too (isabelle://repl/{id} rows), not
  just scope_show's text — needed for T3's "agreement with
  resources/list" to hold at all.
- found and fixed live: MCP.ir (the "repls" fname included) is a
  protocol command defined only by MCP_Repl.thy (the HOL/Ir layer);
  sessions built on the base MCP_Tools.thy alone never register it.
  The first cut called it unconditionally from mcp_resources()/
  scope_show(), which hangs forever on such a session (the promise for
  an unanswered protocol command never completes) — caught by the
  bridge suite (MCP_Bridge_Tests runs over the base "MCP-Tools"
  session) before landing, not by scala-unit (Fake_Backend has no
  protocol layer to hang on). Fixed with
  MCP_Session.repl_bridge_available, a one-time check (is "MCP_Repl"
  in this session's loaded image?) computed at construction, no
  protocol round-trip.
- also lands plans/scope_add's own deferred T5 e2e case (scope_add/
  scope_remove full chain against resources/list + list_changed),
  alongside scope_show's T4, in the same "wave 4" block of
  mcp/test/test_mcp.py.
- all layers green (mcp_test scala-unit + -b bridge, test_mcp.py e2e)
  apart from the 3 pre-existing, unrelated filesystem-tier failures
  already documented in the tool_scope entry below.

2026-07-13 (scope_add/scope_remove: the resource scope lands)
---------------------------------------------------------------

- milestone (spec "scoping"; plans: plans/scope_add, plans/scope_remove,
  both status DONE): the resource scope — a set of theory-name glob
  patterns controlling what resources/list enumerates — is now live,
  scala-side only (MCP_Session.scope_patterns, no ML bridge). scope_add
  adds patterns and replies with each one's current match count against
  the full known theory universe (image/loaded/filesystem tiers, D1's
  theory_map + the session base); scope_remove removes patterns
  literally (the exact string added, not by re-matching), so removing a
  theory name that a glob happened to match is a documented no-op.
  resources/list now lists, tier-tagged, the union of pattern matches
  and the implicit working set (theories loaded via load_theory/
  check_theory, already tracked in theory_master_dirs) — scope still
  never limits resources/read, only the listing. both tools fire
  notifications/resources/list_changed on an actual change, not on
  idempotent no-ops (duplicate add, absent remove). distinct from
  tool_scope_* (2026-07-13, above): that is the agent CONTEXT (which
  tool set is active); this is the RESOURCE scope (which resources
  resources/list enumerates) — same "scope" word, different axis, both
  now implemented and both explicitly cross-reference the distinction
  in code and plans.
- deviation from the plan: S1's third implicit member, active REPLs, is
  not yet folded into resources/list — no scope_add/scope_remove test
  needed it, and it is plans/scope_show's own claim (T1/T2); see
  plans/scope_add's dated deviation note.

2026-07-13 (tool_scope: the agent context lands)
--------------------------------------------------

- milestone (spec phase 3 "visibility", "agent context on the scala
  side" implementation-order item; plan: plans/tool_scope, status
  DONE): the three tool_scope_* builtins (show/set/include) give the
  connection a mutable AGENT CONTEXT — a designation ("" default | a
  bare canonical theory name | "repl:ID") plus an ordered list of
  included bundle names, read on every tools/list and tools/call. the
  self-extension hinge works end to end: a tool registered mid-session
  via repl_step becomes listed and callable once tool_scope_set{repl}
  points the connection at that repl (pinned by a bridge test);
  tool_scope_include opens a bundle (Bundle.includes_cmd, Isar's
  "context includes") to reactivate a scoped-off tool, and the next
  tool_scope_set clears it again ("fresh context, no accumulated
  soup"). mechanics: MCP_Protocol.designated_context grows a repl
  branch resolved through a hook the HOL layer installs
  (MCP_Protocol.set_repl_context_hook, filled with the new
  \<^ML>\<open>Ir.context_of\<close> — additive, in-precedent with ir.ML's
  existing MCP-only hooks); a second wire argument carries the bundle
  list. found and fixed live: MCP.tools/MCP.resources' designation
  resolution was not crash-safe like MCP.run_tool's — an unknown or
  stale (e.g. a removed repl) designation raised an uncaught ML
  exception that left the Scala-side "MCP.tools_result" promise
  unfulfilled forever, confirmed as a real hang by a bridge test
  before the fix (MCP_Protocol.designated_context_safe degrades to an
  empty payload instead); a new MCP.check_designation command lets
  tool_scope_set/include validate a candidate repl/bundle designation
  against the prover BEFORE committing it as connection state (the
  theory case validates for free via resolve_context_theory, which
  also supplies the "normalize before storing" canonical spelling).
  spec refinement: the wire designation stays a bare theory name
  (no "theory:" prefix) rather than growing one, so the shipped
  mcp_tool_registry bridge contract needed no changes; only "repl:ID"
  is new syntax. DEFERRED (decided explicitly rather than folding
  both into one pass): tool_scope_show reflecting builtin activation,
  pending plans/builtin_activation; the Name_Space-extern exposure
  refactor, an internal simplification orthogonal to this surface.
  tool_scope_show separately checks the designation (MCP.check_designation)
  before reading tools, so a scope that went stale after tool_scope_set
  (e.g. its repl was removed) reports itself BROKEN rather than a
  silent "0 active tools" (ml_tools' own crash-safety floor would
  otherwise make the two indistinguishable). all four test layers
  green: ml unit (MCP-Tools-Tests, plus a new MCP_Tool_Scope_Heap_Tests
  fresh-process case for the repl-context resolution mid-proof), scala
  unit (Fake_Backend), bridge (the self-extension hinge and bundle
  scoping against a live MCP-HOL session), e2e (test_mcp.py 79
  cases, only the 3 pre-existing/unrelated filesystem-tier failures
  present, confirmed also present on the unmodified tree via a
  stash/rebuild/retest bisect) — the stdio JSON-RPC wire arity change
  (MCP.tools 1→2 args, MCP.run_tool 4→5) is thereby also e2e-covered,
  not just at the bridge layer.

2026-07-13 (builtin tools join the activation layer)
------------------------------------------------------

- decision (spec phase 3, new section "builtin tools in the
  activation layer"): ONE ACTIVATION LAYER over TWO IMPLEMENTATION
  SUBSTRATES. every scala builtin's name is mirror-registered into
  the ML MCP_Tool registry (new form Builtin, tag "builtin",
  description duplicated, run slot errors — dispatch stays scala-
  side), so the [[mcp_tools add/del: ...]] attribute, bundles,
  print_mcp_tools and the tool_scope_* family govern builtins exactly
  like user tools; the user can prune the ~25-builtin surface to the
  task at hand. mechanics: the MCP.tools payload grows a builtins =
  (name, active) section covering every registered mirror; the scala
  merge serves builtin table rows whose mirror is active. three
  guardrails make the unification safe: (1) AVAILABILITY FLOOR — an
  empty builtins section (designated context without MCP_Tools, no
  ML session, broken -T theory) serves the FULL builtin table, so
  the old two-table merge is the degraded mode and diagnosing a
  broken heap never loses the scala tools; (2) ASYMMETRIC
  CALLABILITY — a del'd builtin is unlisted but still callable
  (activation filters discovery, not access — the resource-scope
  rule; builtins have no alternate route since ML cannot call scala,
  and this makes lockout impossible without a privileged meta-core),
  while a del'd ML tool stays unlisted AND uncallable; (3) DRIFT
  GATE — a bridge test pins mirror name set == builtin table name
  set both ways, so the duplicated list cannot rot. bare-name
  invariant unchanged (mirrors never enter exposure-name
  computation); resources NOT mirrored (they have scope_add).
  while there, the spec's activation examples are corrected to the
  implemented PLURAL attribute spelling ([[mcp_tools ...]]) — the
  old singular examples predated the lexer correction. plan:
  plans/builtin_activation (new, wave: with tool_scope); new
  unchecked item in the spec's phase-3 implementation order;
  plans/tool_scope and plans/mcp_tool_registry annotated.

