2026-07-10 (wave 2: load_theory, unload_theory, check_theory)
---------------------------------------------------------------

- done: wave 2 (plans/load_theory, plans/unload_theory,
  plans/check_theory), on the post-munit-migration testing
  infrastructure. All three tools are scala-side only (session.
  use_theories/purge_theories over Headless), disjoint from the MCP.ir
  bridge -- Ir.load_theory's headless refusal is deliberately left in
  place, see the KNOWN GAP test in mcp_bridge_tests.scala.
- finding, pinned (a correction of the plan's own assumption): plans/
  check_theory's "PURGE BEFORE RE-LOADING" is empirically WRONG for
  this Headless API and actively corrupts the document if followed
  literally. Headless.Resources.purge_theories only updates its own
  Scala-side bookkeeping and never pushes purge_edits via
  session.update, so a manual purge desyncs Resources' record of a
  node's prior content from what the live prover document still holds;
  the next use_theories then diffs the new file content against a
  phantom "no prior content" baseline and inserts it on top of the
  still-present old text -- observed directly as duplicated theory
  headers and cascading "missing theory context" outer-syntax errors.
  The actual fix: check_theory does NOT purge at all -- session.
  use_theories() already reads the file fresh and diffs against its
  OWN correctly-tracked prior content on every call, so a plain re-run
  already detects on-disk edits with no purge needed (mcp_session.
  scala's check_theory comment; three-beat bridge test T1). unload_
  theory, which does need an actual document-level removal (not just a
  fresh read), goes through Resources.clean_theories instead --
  unload_theories + purge_theories(None) + session.update in one
  state.change -- the one purge path that keeps its own bookkeeping in
  sync with the live document.
- infra: unload_theory's inputSchema is just {name} (no master_dir),
  but resolving a theory to its Document.Node.Name needs the master_dir
  it was loaded with. MCP_Session now records name -> master_dir on
  every successful load_theory/check_theory call, so unload_theory can
  resolve consistently without asking for it again; it also doubles as
  the "was this ever loaded" registry for unload_theory's not-loaded
  error path.
- tests: bridge suite (mcp_bridge_tests.scala) covers load_theory T1
  (well-formed fixture under master_dir), T2 (broken fixture ->
  line-positioned isError, session survives), T4 (idempotent re-load);
  unload_theory T1 (load/unload/load-fresh cycle); check_theory T1 (the
  staleness three-beat), T2 (never-loaded purge no-op path), T3
  (warning-vs-error policy). e2e (test_mcp.py) threads load_theory ->
  check_theory staleness -> unload_theory -> a flagship approximation
  of plans/check_theory T4 (repl proves a lemma, repl_text extracts the
  script, python splices it into a fixture file, check_theory confirms
  it on disk) -- the true attach step (repl_init_from_source) is still
  deferred, so the REPL proves independently rather than attaching to
  the loaded theory's document.
- scala-unit coverage (mcp_handler_tests.scala, Fake_Backend) predates
  this entry (added during the munit migration): tools/list rows,
  dispatch-not-ir assertions, unload_theory's two error paths.

