- done: sledgehammer implemented (plans/sledgehammer, wave 1 — the
  flagship async proof-search call) — builtin table row (fname
  "sledgehammer", {readOnlyHint: true, idempotentHint: false} -- the
  repl state is untouched but ATP results vary run to run); first
  tool with an OPTIONAL schema property (timeout_secs, default 15,
  not in "required"). No special handler code was needed for the
  omission: json_args already only emits pairs for keys present in
  the client's arguments object, so omitting timeout_secs reaches
  backend.ir as exactly [(repl,T)] and the ML dispatcher's own
  default applies untouched — confirmed by T1.
- fixed: a genuine, previously-undiscovered bug in ir.ML surfaced by
  actually exercising sledgehammer end to end for the first time.
  Ir.sledgehammer's deferred ML_Compiler.eval (needed because the
  Sledgehammer structures live in the HOL heap, unavailable at ir.ML
  compile time) picked its compilation context via
  `Toplevel.theory_of st` — the REPL's OWN base theory (e.g. plain
  "Main"). Main is an ANCESTOR of MCP_Repl (which declares the Ir
  structure via ML_file), not a descendant, so Main's theory-local ML
  environment does not see Ir — every real call failed with
  "Structure (Ir) has not been declared". Fixed by capturing
  MCP_Repl's own theory value ONCE at load time
  (Ir.set_self_theory, called from MCP_Repl.thy right after
  `ML_file "ir.ML"`) and using that captured value as the eval
  context instead; MCP_Repl imports Main, so its own theory is a
  strict superset (has both Sledgehammer and Ir). A first attempt
  used `Thy_Info.get_theory "MCP_Repl"` (a by-name lookup) instead of
  a captured value; that works under batch `isabelle build` but fails
  against a live headless PIDE session (MCP_Session.start) with
  "Theory loader: undefined entry for theory ..." — the same class of
  gap already on record for load_theory (plans/repl_init T6,
  plans/load_theory): such sessions load theories through the
  document model, not the classical Thy_Info loader. Capturing the
  theory value directly sidesteps the loader and works uniformly in
  both contexts.
- decided (T4, concurrency): sledgehammer's ML_Compiler.eval bridge
  passes arguments through two shared Synchronized slots
  (sledgehammer_state/sledgehammer_result), so two CONCURRENT
  sledgehammer calls would race them. Serialized via a new global
  blocking lock (Ir.with_sledgehammer_lock, ir.ML) rather than
  rejecting the second caller with a busy error — the dispatcher
  already runs each request in its own fork_run future, and a short
  wait is simpler for callers than a distinct error to retry on. The
  lock only serializes among sledgehammer calls; other REPL
  operations are unaffected (confirmed by T5, a fast repl_list call
  during an in-flight sledgehammer).
  step 1's prover inventory (isabelle components -l): cvc5-1.2.0-1,
  spass-3.8ds-2, vampire-4.8, verit-2021.06.2-rmx-3, z3-4.4.0pre-4 all
  bundled. T2 confirmed a fresh REPL at theory level (not mid-proof)
  fails sledgehammer cleanly with a status error. T3 (tolerant of
  prover flakiness) found a proof for a trivial nat-commutativity goal
  and the suggested one-liner closed it via a subsequent step. T6
  (e2e) ran the full model-facing loop — step a lemma, sledgehammer,
  step the suggestion — in test_mcp.py (test_agentic.py does not
  exist in this repo, so e2e coverage lives alongside the other
  builtins' e2e tests there). ml unit: MCP_Repl_Tests.thy; scala unit:
  isabelle mcp_test; bridge (T3-T5): isabelle mcp_test -b -d mcp/Tools;
  e2e: test_mcp.py. all layers green.

