- done: repl_timeout implemented (plans/repl_timeout, wave 1) —
  builtin table row (fname "timeout", pass-through {repl, secs} ->
  backend.ir("timeout", [(repl,...),(secs,...)])); mutating but
  genuinely idempotent (setting the same value twice is a no-op), so
  {idempotentHint: true} within the mutating bucket, same class as
  repl_replay. T1 confirmed the set value bounds steps: secs=1 makes a
  2s sleeping step time out, secs=0 lets the same step run to
  completion. T2 (pinned, a genuine surprise): exec_text only bounds
  execution when timeout_secs > 0, so a negative value behaves exactly
  like 0/unlimited for STEP EXECUTION -- but repl_show's rendering only
  special-cases exactly 0 as "unlimited"; any other value, negative
  included, goes through SML's plain string_of_int, which spells
  negative numbers with a tilde, not a minus sign, so a REPL set to -1
  shows "timeout=~1s", not "timeout=-1s". Both halves (execution
  treats negative as unlimited; display does not, and spells it oddly)
  are pinned in the test rather than silently "fixed", since this is
  existing ir.ML behavior, not something this tool introduced. T3
  confirmed the normal rendering: "timeout=Ns" / "timeout=unlimited".
  T4 exercised the full chain at both the bridge and e2e layers against
