- done: repl_merge implemented (plans/repl_merge, wave 1) — builtin
  table row (fname "merge", pass-through {repl} (the CHILD, parent
  implied by origin) -> backend.ir("merge", [(repl,...)])); mutating
  and destructive ({destructiveHint: true} — deletes the child, can
  replace a parent step). T1 pinned both merge shapes: forking at the
  parent's tip and merging APPENDS the child's concatenated steps as
  one new step ("appended as new step"); forking at an interior state
  and merging REPLACES the step at the fork index ("replaced step N")
  -- the tail-staleness half of the interior case was dropped from the
  test for the same auto_replay=true reason as repl_edit/repl_show (the
  tail is always replayed immediately, no [stale] mark is ever
  observable on this server). T2 confirmed merging a non-sub-REPL
  errors "is not a sub-REPL" and the REPL is otherwise untouched. T3
  confirmed full atomicity on a parent-side failure: fork, then edit
  the parent's pre-fork step (moving the goal out from under the
  child's already-recorded tactic), then merge -- it fails and BOTH
  REPLs survive completely unchanged, the same release-the-original-
  snapshot behavior already pinned for repl_replay T3. T4 (bridge)
  confirmed a busy parent blocks merge without corrupting the child --
  concurrent merge during a slow parent step errors "busy", nothing is
  deleted, and a retried merge succeeds once the parent frees up.
